# Exercise: Measuring a Duration
#
# `time.nanos` returns a high-resolution monotonic clock value.
# The number itself is meaningless — there's no defined zero
# point — but the *difference* between two readings is the
# elapsed time in nanoseconds.
#
# Stack effect: ( -- Int )
#
# The idiom:
#
#   time.nanos       # ( before )
#   do-some-work
#   time.nanos       # ( before after )
#   swap i.-         # ( after - before ) → elapsed nanoseconds
#
# Why monotonic and not `time.now`? Wall clocks can jump
# backwards (NTP corrections, daylight savings, the user editing
# the system clock). `time.nanos` only ever moves forward, so a
# duration computed from it is always non-negative.
#
# Your task: write `monotonic-non-decreasing?` — read
# `time.nanos` twice in a row and return true when the second
# reading is greater than or equal to the first. By construction
# this should always be true; the test is verifying you got the
# call order and comparison direction right.
#
# Stack hint: `i.<=` is the integer "less than or equal".
# Reading is left-to-right: `a b i.<=` returns `a <= b`.
#
# When done, delete the marker line below.

# I AM NOT DONE

: monotonic-non-decreasing? ( -- Bool )
    # Your code here
    false
;

: test-monotonic ( -- )
    monotonic-non-decreasing?

    # Do not edit below this line
    test.assert
;
